<h1 class="h3 mb-3">About Transit Explorer</h1>

<p>
  Transit Explorer is a Ruby on Rails app for exploring Winnipeg Transit stops and routes.
  Existing transit apps tell you when the next bus arrives. This app is about the stops
  themselves: which routes serve them, which streets they are on, and whether they have
  a shelter.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Data sources</h2>
<p>
  All data comes from the <a href="https://api.winnipegtransit.com">Winnipeg Transit API</a> (v4),
  requested as JSON. Three separate endpoints are used:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>routes</strong> (<code>routes.json</code>): every route's number and name.</li>
  <li><strong>stops</strong> (<code>stops.json?route=N</code>): the stops on each loaded route, with
    name, direction, side, street, cross street and latitude/longitude.</li>
  <li><strong>stop features</strong> (<code>stops/{number}/features.json</code>): the features at each stop,
    such as shelters and benches.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  <code>db/seeds.rb</code> loads routes, streets, stops and the route-to-stop links, and the rake task
  <code>rails transit:features</code> fills in the shelter and bench flags. The API's own IDs are kept in
  separate columns (<code>transit_id</code>, <code>stop_number</code>, <code>route_number</code>), and
  Rails creates its own primary and foreign keys.
</p>
<p>
  Currently loaded:
  <strong><%= Stop.count %></strong> stops,
  <strong><%= Street.count %></strong> streets,
  <strong><%= Route.count %></strong> routes and
  <strong><%= RouteStop.count %></strong> route-stop links.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Database tables</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>streets</strong>: <code>transit_id</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>street_type</code></li>
  <li><strong>routes</strong>: <code>route_number</code>, <code>name</code></li>
  <li><strong>stops</strong>: <code>stop_number</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>direction</code>, <code>side</code>,
    <code>latitude</code>, <code>longitude</code>, <code>has_shelter</code>, <code>has_bench</code>,
    <code>departures_per_day</code>, and foreign keys <code>street_id</code> and <code>cross_street_id</code></li>
  <li><strong>route_stops</strong>: join table with <code>route_id</code> and <code>stop_id</code></li>
</ul>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">How the data is related</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A street has many stops, as the stop's street or as its cross street (one-to-many).</li>
  <li>A route has many stops and a stop is served by many routes, through <code>route_stops</code> (many-to-many).</li>
</ul>
<p>
  This lets you browse hierarchically: from a route to its stops, from a stop to its street,
  and from a street to all the stops on it.
</p>

<h2 class="h5 mt-4">Stop quality score (in progress)</h2>
<p>
  The planned score rates each stop out of 100: up to 30 points for the number of routes serving it,
  up to 40 for departures per day, 15 for a shelter and 5 for a bench. Shelter and bench data are
  already loaded from the stop features endpoint. Departure frequency has not been loaded yet,
  so stops show as "Not rated yet".
</p>

